Proclaimed in 1994, Black River Gorges is the largest national park in Mauritius, covering about 67 km² of humid upland forest, drier lowland forest and marshy heathland. Its ravines run down from the central plateau towards the west coast at Black River.

The park is the last refuge of much of the island's native wildlife. It is home to all of its surviving endemic bird species — among them the Mauritius kestrel, the pink pigeon and the echo parakeet — which were brought back from the edge of extinction.

Some 60 km of trails cross the park, from short walks to long descents into the gorges. They start both near the coast at Black River and up on the plateau, around Plaine Champagne and the Gorges Viewpoint. The highest summit in Mauritius, the Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire (828 m), rises above the park.

## Good to know

- Set off early, carry water and wear proper shoes: some trails are steep and slippery after rain.
- Tell someone your route before a long hike, and keep to the marked paths.
